EXT. GLACIER CREVASSE CAMP - DAWN

A tent the color of a tangerine, pegged to blue ice. Wind has dropped. The world is pearl and silent.

HILDA (50s, frost in her eyebrows) kneels at a camp stove, coaxing a flame. SOLVEIG (50s, same gray parka, same cropped silver hair) unrolls a map across her knees. JAMAL (20s, hauling a coil of rope) crunches up from the edge, face wind-reddened.

JAMAL
Weather's holding.

HILDA
Good. Then we eat.

She stirs a pot. Steam wisps. Solveig traces a finger across the map and does not look up.

SOLVEIG
Jamal, how far to the saddle?

JAMAL
Two hours if the bridge holds.

SOLVEIG
Hilda, did you check the bridge?

HILDA
Solveig, I checked it yesterday. She was fine.

JAMAL
(confused)
She?

Hilda and Solveig exchange a look. Hilda's smile fades. Solveig folds the map once.

SOLVEIG
Jamal. Where is the third rope?

Jamal glances at the coil in his hand. Then at the edge. A beat.

JAMAL
I left it anchored. For her.

Solveig stands. Hilda lowers the spoon. She looks at the pot, at the steam, at the tent, then at Jamal, who has begun, very slowly, to back away toward the crevasse.

HILDA
(gentle)
Jamal. There wasn't a third climber.

He stops. The wind returns, and with it, faintly, from deep inside the ice, a woman's voice calling his name.
